me><xref ref-type="aff" rid="a">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Monzingo</surname><given-names initials="AF">Arthur F.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="a">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Robertus</surname><given-names initials="JD">Jon D.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="a">a</xref><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor">*</xref></contrib><aff id="a"><label>a</label>Institute for Cellular and Molecular Biology, Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, University of Texas, 1 University Station A5300, Austin, TX 78712, <country>USA</country></aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor">Correspondence e-mail: <email>jrobertus@mail.utexas.edu</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><day>01</day><month>1</month><year>2009</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>19</day><month>12</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><volume>65</volume><issue>Pt 1</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="pmc-issue-id">176797</issue-id><issue-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">d090100</issue-id><fpage>11</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>04</day><month>9</month><year>2008</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>07</day><month>10</month><year>2008</year></date></history><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>01</day><month>01</month><year>2010</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>05</day><month>03</month><year>2009</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2026-08-26 22:25:17.367"><day>26</day><month>08</month><year>2026</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><copyright-statement>© International Union of Crystallography 2009</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2009</copyright-year></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="d-65-00011.pdf"/><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/10.1107/S0907444908032186">A full version of this article is available from Crystallography Journals Online.</self-uri><abstract abstract-type="toc"><p>The structure of the effector domain of the influenza protein NS1, a validated antiviral drug target, has been solved in two space groups.</p></abstract><abstract><p>The nonstructural protein NS1A from influenza virus is a multifunctional virulence factor and a potent inhibitor of host immunity. It has two functional domains: an N-terminal 73-­amino-acid RNA-binding domain and a C-terminal effector domain. Here, the crystallographic structure of the NS1A effector domain of influenza A/Udorn/72 virus is presented. Structure comparison with the NS1 effector domain from mouse-adapted influenza A/Puerto Rico/8/34 (PR8) virus strain reveals a similar monomer conformation but a different dimer interface. Further analysis and evaluation shows that the dimer interface observed in the structure of the PR8 NS1 effector domain is likely to be a crystallo­graphic packing effect. A hypothetical model of the intact NS1 dimer is presented.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>influenza NS1</kwd><kwd>effector domain</kwd><kwd>dimer formation</kwd><kwd>protein–protein interactions</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-live</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-embargo</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-released</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-open-access</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-olf</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-prop-manuscript</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-